        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Ain Jalut</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">3 September 1260</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the spring of Ain Jalut at the northwestern foot of Mount Gilboa, in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezreel-valley">Jezreel Valley</a>, about 15 km north-northwest of Baysan</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Sultan Qutuz</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> commanding the vanguard; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>'s army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a>, between 10,000 and 12,000 men by one estimate, with Georgian, Armenian and Ayyubid contingents</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Mongol force was driven back and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> died with most of the army that remained in the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> five days later and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> within a month.</span></div>
        </div>
