    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hulegu left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with the bulk of his army after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a>, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> a far smaller force when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> advanced from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> used a smaller force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> to draw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> into the highlands, where the rest of their army waited in hiding.</li><li>Hulegu could not avenge the defeat, in part because of his quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> killed Hulegu's envoys and displayed their heads on Bab Zuweila, one of the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, before he marched. He was himself assassinated on the way back to Cairo after the victory, in a conspiracy led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, who became sultan.</p></aside>
    </section>

