    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1258</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu's army captures and sacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> is destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu's envoys demand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a>'s surrender, and Qutuz kills them and displays their heads at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu withdraws with the bulk of his army, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> about one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumen</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 26 July, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> takes Gaza</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Mongol armies meet at Ain Jalut on 3 September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> falls to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> five days later and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> within a month; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> is assassinated and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> becomes sultan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1260</span><span class="rf-text">A Mongol force of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in December and massacres many Muslims, but withdraws after a fortnight</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1263</span><span class="rf-text">Hulegu is defeated north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> in the first open war among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-ain-jalut" data-v="ecafbfd832" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Mongols hold the spring</b> Kitbuqa reached Ain Jalut first and camped by the spring, with Mount Gilboa covering one flank. Baibars, leading the Mamluk vanguard, sighted the camp from a height, while Qutuz hid the bulk of his army in the highlands.</li><li><b>The Mongols advance and Baibars strikes and runs</b> The Mongols moved first. For many hours Baibars provoked them with hit-and-run attacks and kept most of his men out of the fight.</li><li><b>Baibars feigns a final retreat</b> After another heavy Mongol assault Baibars and his men pretended to break and rode back towards the highlands. The aim was to draw the Mongols after them into an ambush.</li><li><b>Kitbuqa follows with all his troops</b> Kitbuqa, already provoked by the constant flight of Baibars and his men, did not suspect a trick. He marched forward with all of his troops on the trail of the fleeing Mamluks.</li><li><b>The trap closes</b> When the Mongols reached the highlands the hidden Mamluks came out, shooting arrows and charging with their cavalry. The Mongols found themselves surrounded.</li><li><b>The Mongols fight to break out</b> The Mongol army fought aggressively to break out of the ring. Their attack almost destroyed the Mamluk left wing, which gave way before it.</li><li><b>Qutuz throws away his helmet and charges</b> Qutuz saw the left wing almost destroyed. He threw away his combat helmet so that his warriors could recognise him, then rushed towards the weakened side, followed by his own unit.</li><li><b>The Mongols are pushed back towards Baysan</b> The Mongols were pushed back by the Mamluk attack and fled to the neighbourhood of Baysan. Qutuz’s forces followed them eastwards across the field.</li><li><b>The Mongols reorganise and counter-attack</b> The Mongols managed to reorganise and returned to the battlefield, where they made a successful counter-attack.</li><li><b>The battle turns and Kitbuqa dies</b> The battle then shifted towards the Mamluks, who held the advantage of ground and morale. Some of the Mongols were forced to retreat, and Kitbuqa perished with almost all the rest of the army that remained in the region.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongke.jpg" alt="Möngke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%B6ngke_enthroned._Tarikh-i_Jahangushay-i_Juvaini,_1438,_Shiraz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1438 artist, Shiraz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1251 and set out to carry out his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s plan for a world empire. He chose his brother Hulegu to subdue the nations of the west. Assembling the army took five years, and Hulegu was ready to begin only in 1256. Möngke ordered good treatment for those who yielded without resistance and destruction for the rest. Other countries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>' path submitted and contributed forces, so that by the time the army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> it included Cilician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and some Frankish troops from the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> fell, the 500-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> of Baghdad was destroyed in 1258, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> fell as well. Hulegu's plan was then to move south through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, the major Islamic power.</p><p>In 1260 Hulegu sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> with a letter demanding his surrender. Qutuz killed the envoys and displayed their heads on Bab Zuweila. Möngke Khan was slain in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a>, and Hulegu returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> with the bulk of his army to attend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> in accordance with Mongol custom. He left approximately 10,000 troops west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a>. Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>'s supply of Kipchaks replenished the Mamluk armies.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Ain Jalut&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Reuven Amitai-Preiss, Mongols and Mamluks: The Mamluk-Ilkhanid War, 1260–1281 (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1995)</li><li>Peter Thorau, The Lion of Egypt : Sultan Baybars I and the Near East in the thirteenth century (Longman, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mamluk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamluk.jpg" alt="Mamluk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Mamelukes_with_lances_on_horseback.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniel Hopfer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> was a Naiman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christian</a> general. The army left to him was one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumen</a>, nominally 10,000 men but usually fewer. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> chronicler al-Yunini gives the Mongol army with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> as 100,000 men, a figure that is likely an exaggeration. Kitbuqa's strength is put at between 10,000 and 12,000 soldiers, including Georgian and Lesser Armenian battalions (about 500 Cilician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>), the contingents of two Ayyubid princes, al-Ashraf Musa and al-Said Hasan, and local levies that had served the Syrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> assembled a large army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and left on 26 July 1260 for al-Salihiyya. It was made up of Egyptian troops, the Syrian battalions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-yusuf">al-Nasir Yusuf</a>, Shahraziriyya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Turcomans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">Al-Maqrizi</a> claims that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> at al-Salihiyya declined to attack the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> until Qutuz overcame their opposition with a powerful speech. He put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> at the head of the vanguard, and Baybars took Gaza, a Mongol outpost, before its commander Baydara could receive orders from Kitbuqa. The route north ran through Frankish territory. The rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, after lengthy discussion, stayed neutral, let Qutuz pass and supplied his army on the plain of Acre.</p><p>Kitbuqa chose to stand at Ain Jalut, a perennial spring at the northwestern foot of Mount Gilboa, where the Wadi Jalut gave water for horses, the valley offered pasture and good ground for cavalry, and the slopes of Gilboa secured a flank. The evidence strongly indicates that the Mongols arrived first and took up their positions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongols.jpg" alt="Mongols" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_entre_mongols_%26_chinois_(1211).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sayf al-Vâhidî. Hérât. Afghanistan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> advanced first. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> knew the ground, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> hid the bulk of his force in the highlands and meant to bait the Mongols with a smaller force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>. Both armies fought for many hours, with Baybars using hit-and-run tactics to provoke the Mongols while keeping the bulk of his troops intact. When the Mongols made another heavy assault, Baybars and his men feigned a final retreat to draw them into the highlands, where the rest of the Mamluk force was concealed among the trees. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a>, already provoked by the repeated flight of Baybars's men, advanced with all his troops. The Mamluks then emerged, shot arrows and attacked with their cavalry, and the Mongols were surrounded on all sides. Timothy May hypothesises that a key moment was the defection of the Mongols' Syrian allies.</p><p>The Mongols fought hard to break out. Qutuz watched with his private legion, and when he saw the left wing of the Mamluk army almost destroyed by the Mongol attack he threw away his helmet so that his men could recognise him and cried out three times to God for victory. He then rushed towards the weakened side, shouting wa islamah, followed by his own unit. The Mongols were pushed back and fled towards Beisan, followed by Qutuz's forces. They reorganised and returned to make a successful counterattack, but the battle shifted towards the Mamluks, who now held the advantage of ground and morale, and some of the Mongols were forced to retreat. Kitbuqa, with almost all the rest of the Mongol army that had remained in the region, perished.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baybars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baybars.jpg" alt="Baybars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baybars,_Medallion_IV,_Baptist%C3%A8re_de_Saint-Louis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">14th century artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hulegu ordered the execution of the last Ayyubid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, an-Nasir Yusuf, and his brother, who were in captivity, when he heard of the defeat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> captured Damascus five days after Ain Jalut and Aleppo within a month. On the way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> was assassinated by several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> in a conspiracy led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, who became the new sultan. Local Ayyubid emirs sworn to the Mamluk sultanate then defeated another Mongol force of 6,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, which ended the first Mongol expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>Hulegu could not bring his full power against the Mamluks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, was horrified at the destruction of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> quotes him as writing that Hulegu had sacked all the cities of the Muslims and caused the death of the caliph. The Mamluks learned through spies that Berke was a Muslim and no friend of his cousin, and cultivated ties with him. In December 1260 Hulegu sent two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a> to attack the Mamluks in Aleppo. They massacred a large number of Muslims in retaliation for the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> but, after a fortnight, made no further progress and withdrew. Hulegu returned to his lands by 1262 and massed his armies, but Berke's raids lured him north, and he suffered a severe defeat in 1263 in an invasion north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, the first open war among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. He died in 1265 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani.jpg" alt="Rashid al-Din Hamadani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presentation_of_the_book,_folio_6_(Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France,_Suppl%C3%A9ment_Persan_1113).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jami al-Tawarikh artist, 1420-1430 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and Persian writers. Al-Yunini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> are the sources for the Mamluk side, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a>'s message. The large number of sources in vastly different languages has led Mongol historians to focus on limited aspects of the empire. Studies of the battle include those by Amitai-Preiss, Halperin and John Masson Smith, whose 1984 paper is titled "Ayn Jalut: Mamluk Success or Mongol Failure?".</p><p>Several points are uncertain or disputed. Strengths are known only as estimates: al-Yunini's 100,000 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> is likely an exaggeration. That <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> laid out the overall strategy is only said to be so, because he had spent time in the region as a fugitive. The sources differ on where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> was camped before the battle: Rashid al-Din has him at Ba'labakk and al-Yunini on the Biqa plain. May's suggestion about the defection of Syrian allies is a hypothesis. Ahmad Yousef al-Hassan holds that Mamluk hand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> were used, which would make it the earliest known use; David Ayalon, Iqtidar Alam Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-needham">Joseph Needham</a>, Tonio Andrade and Gabor Ágoston contradict this, and Khan believes cannon reached Mamluk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> only in the 1370s. A recent study argues that a short climate anomaly after the Samalas eruption may have contributed to a Mongol withdrawal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle has been described by numerous academic and popular historians as epochal: the first time a Mongol advance met a major defeat and a permanent halt. It was the first of two defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> suffered in trying to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, the other being the Battle of Marj al-Saffar in 1303. Beside Ain Jalut the Mongols were beaten at the second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbistan">Elbistan</a> and Marj al-Saffar; in five battles with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> they won only at Wadi al-Khaznadar, and they never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> and his successors went on to capture the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> by 1291.</p><p>The defeat also coincided with the end of the unified Mongol empire, since the war between Hulegu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> was the first open war among the Mongols. In later memory the name has been taken up in modern politics: one of the three original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> Liberation Army was named Ain Jalut, and in July 1970 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasser-arafat">Yasser Arafat</a> referred to the battle, saying that the Mongols who had swept away the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> were defeated at Ain Jalut, in the same region where he said his people were then fighting the Zionists.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Ain Jalut&#8221; cites where it covers Medieval, Modern:</p><ul><li>Joseph Needham, Chemistry and Chemical Technology, Part 7, Military Technology: The Gunpowder Epic (Cambridge University Press, 1986)</li><li>Iqtidar Alam Khan, Gunpowder and Firearms: Warfare in Medieval India (Oxford University Press, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 3 September 1260 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> met a Mongol force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> at Ain Jalut and drove it back, killing Kitbuqa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> never returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</em></p>

